# Copy to .env and fill in.
#
# Auth is the owner's Claude subscription (Pro/Max): run `claude setup-token`
# once (1-year, inference-only OAuth token) and paste it here. The control
# plane injects it into run-bursts as the container's only Anthropic credential.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Fallback only — API billing. NOTE: if both are set, the runner injects the
# OAuth token and ignores this (the CLI itself would prefer the API key).
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# GitHub App (the dev's scoped identity). The private key never leaves the
# control plane; each burst gets a fresh 1-hour installation token.
# App ID + key: the App's settings page. Installation ID: optional — discovered
# automatically when the App has exactly one installation. The key variable
# is a secret ref (D4): a plain path, or env:VAR / file:PATH / helper:NAME —
# the same for every role's *_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH below.
GITHUB_APP_ID=
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=
# GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=

# Machine review (REVIEWERS.md): a toggle and a selector, everything declared.
# REVIEW=on requires REVIEW_PROVIDERS to name at least one provider; a declaration
# that can't be honored (unknown name, missing credential) refuses to start.
# Providers: copilot (shipped) · claude, codex (REVIEWERS.md slices 2–3).
# These are also `product register`'s defaults (C1): registration materializes
# them into the product's row, and changing them here never touches a live row.
# Registration preflights the declaration (C2): a provider declared without its
# credential below refuses to register — the startup error, asked earlier.
# At scheduler startup a registered product reads its row, not these (C3) — the
# toggle+selector here govern only an unregistered repo (dev mode). Credentials
# stay machine-level and apply to both; the reviewer's model is its role row's
# (`role set reviewer --model`, D5).
# REVIEW=off
# REVIEW_PROVIDERS=

# Credentials for the `claude` provider: a second GitHub App (the PR's author
# can't review it, so findings post under a distinct identity). Permissions:
# Pull requests read/write only, installed on the product repo. The reviewer
# burst itself gets NO GitHub credential — the control plane posts for it.
# REVIEWER_GITHUB_APP_ID=
# REVIEWER_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=

# Role-prefixed App credentials (TO-BE §4, D1): one App per pipeline role, the
# same three names behind each prefix (the dev App reads them bare, above).
# `control-plane github-check` preflights every configured role. Test reports
# and relayed auto issues post under the tester App (D3) — E2E=on requires it.
# MONITOR_ is reserved — its App arrives at T4.
# TESTER_GITHUB_APP_ID=
# TESTER_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=

# Credential for the `copilot` provider: GitHub grants Copilot review requests
# only to a *user* token whose owner has a Copilot seat — an App/bot token gets
# HTTP 201 and the reviewer is silently dropped (verified 2026-07-03, PR #6).
# Fine-grained PAT of the owner: repository = the product repo, permission =
# Pull requests read/write. Used by the control plane for review requests only;
# never enters the burst container.
# GITHUB_COPILOT_REVIEW_TOKEN=

# E2E testing (TESTER.md): E2E=on runs a tester burst after a clean machine
# review, when the product ships a TESTING.md. Requires the claude review
# provider (the test stage triggers on its APPROVE). Like REVIEW, this is a
# register default (C1) and the dev-mode toggle — a registered product reads
# its row (C3). The tester's model is its role row's (`role set tester --model`).
E2E=off

# Sandbox credentials for tester bursts. A registered product declares each as
# a secret ref on its row (`product set --test-env DISCORD_TOKEN=env:TEST_DISCORD_TOKEN`)
# and the SecretResolver reads it at spawn (D4); only names the product's
# TESTING.md asks for AND the row declares are injected (as the unprefixed
# name), into tester bursts only. An unregistered, env-governed run reads the
# TEST_ prefix instead: X ← env:TEST_X. Provision disposable resources: for
# discord-bot, a dedicated test app + empty guild.
# TEST_DISCORD_TOKEN=
# TEST_GUILD_ID=

# Secret refs (TO-BE §4, D4). Every secret the platform holds is a ref, never a
# value: env:VAR, file:PATH, or helper:NAME. The helper is one binary you point
# at here — invoked as `<helper> get NAME`, value on stdout (the
# git-credential-helper shape) — and is how a vault, an agent or anything else
# plugs in without the platform knowing its name. Unset ⇒ helper: refs refuse.
# SECRET_HELPER=/usr/local/bin/platform-secret

# Per-role execution parameters are NOT env (D5, TO-BE §4): the model a role's
# bursts run on, its per-burst budget (the SDK's HARD cap on estimated cost —
# it kills the burst mid-run; resume makes retries continue, not restart), its
# max turns, its prompt preamble and its mount are the role *row's*, read at
# every spawn:
#   control-plane role list
#   control-plane role set dev --model claude-sonnet-5 --budget 2.0 --turns 100
# Seeded: claude-sonnet-5 / $2.00 / 100 turns for every pipeline role — the pin
# exists so "unset" never means "whatever `claude` in the image defaults to".
# DEV_AGENT_MODEL, REVIEWER_MODEL, TESTER_MODEL, SCHEDULER_BURST_BUDGET_USD and
# SCHEDULER_BURST_MAX_TURNS are retired: set, they make startup refuse.

# Scheduler rails (SCHEDULER.md) — hard limits on unattended behavior.
# Defaults shown; uncomment to override. The daily ceiling is per product: a
# registered row's daily_bursts column governs it (C4) — this env value is the
# `product register` default (C1) and the dev-mode rail.
# SCHEDULER_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# SCHEDULER_MAX_REVIEW_ROUNDS=3
# SCHEDULER_DAILY_BURST_CEILING=20
# SCHEDULER_FAILURE_COOLDOWN_S=300
# SCHEDULER_POLL_INTERVAL_S=60
# How long a replica's claim on a task holds without a heartbeat (E1): the
# longest a dead replica's work stays owned before a peer reconciles it. The
# heartbeat renews every third of it while the container runs.
# SCHEDULER_LEASE_S=90

# Tracing (ARCHITECTURE.md §9): set to export burst spans via OTLP. Port 4318 is
# an OTLP/HTTP collector's; a bare Phoenix listens on 6006 and takes traces only,
# which is why the dev rig puts a collector in front —
# `docker compose -f docker/compose.telemetry.yaml up -d --wait`, and
# OBSERVABILITY.md for the rest of the wiring.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318

# Where a *burst* pushes its own telemetry (B2) — Claude Code's traces and tool
# events, stamped with task/burst/role/kind. A separate variable from the one
# above because the container is in another network namespace: `localhost` inside
# the box is the box, and the CLI drops export failures silently. Unset ⇒ no
# telemetry env enters the container.
# DEV_AGENT_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:4318

# Webhooks as wake-up (E5, TO-BE §5) — polls stay the truth; these only make the
# next poll happen now. Both unset ⇒ polls only, the laptop's shape.
#
# On a replica: host:port for the internal wake endpoint (`POST /wake`,
# `GET /healthz`). Internal and unauthenticated by design — the worst a caller
# can cause is one poll of real GitHub state — so never route it from outside.
# WAKE_BIND=0.0.0.0:8081
#
# The receiver's side (`webhook-receiver serve`, its own process and image) is
# its own file, .env.receiver — see .env.receiver.example. Nothing in this file
# reaches the receiver: it is the one public process, and what it can leak is
# what it is handed.

# The two store toggles (STORES.md), independent of each other because a
# deployment can buy one capability before the other. Neither is a mode flag —
# each is the same design with and without a service behind it.
#
# The ledger: set it and the ledger is Postgres, whose schema
# `control-plane migrate` owns; unset, it is the single-node SQLite file under
# --data-dir, which needs no migration. The dev rig's DSN, connecting as the
# schema owner (docker/compose.yaml):
# LEDGER_DSN=postgresql://platform_admin:platform@localhost:5432/platform?sslmode=disable
#
# The session archive: set it and snapshots go to an S3 bucket; unset, they go to
# a directory under --data-dir, with the same keys. `tls=false` is this store's
# `sslmode=disable`; the bucket is created on first write if it doesn't exist.
# Credentials belong to the control plane alone — no burst ever holds them.
# ARCHIVE_URL=s3://platform:platform-dev-secret@localhost:8333/platform-sessions?tls=false
#
# Where bursts run (G3), the third toggle of the same kind: set it and each burst
# is a k8s Job in that namespace — one pod, three containers, no docker socket
# and no service-account token in it; unset, bursts are containers on this host's
# docker, as before. A cluster needs ARCHIVE_URL too: a pod moves its own bytes
# through presigned URLs, and a directory has none to hand out. DEPLOYMENT.md,
# "Bursts on a cluster", is the rest; `scripts/burst-cluster.sh` builds a
# throwaway one.
# BURST_NAMESPACE=bursts
# BURST_PHASES_IMAGE=usf/burst-phases:latest
